Based on your setup and goals (DeepL API v2, Node.js/Express, SLA <300ms, heavy focus on latency), here’s a lean approach that consistently keeps responses fast.

What to do
- Avoid a separate language-detect call on the critical path. Let DeepL auto-detect source_lang; call detection only when you need to display the detected language. This removes ~50–100ms.
- Coalesce duplicate in-flight requests. If the same text/target arrives concurrently, serve all from a single DeepL call.
- Two-tier caching:
  - In-process LRU for ultra-hot hits (sub-millisecond returns).
  - Redis for cross-instance hits. Use a stable key (hash of text + target_lang [+ source_lang if you ever pass it]).
  - Add a short TTL with jitter (e.g., 5–30 min + random 0–10%) and size guards to avoid caching huge payloads.
- Batch when possible. DeepL supports multiple text entries in one request; if your UI submits several segments, send them in one call to cut overhead.
- Keep the DB write off the hot path. Return the translation as soon as you have it; persist to DB asynchronously (queue or fire-and-forget).
- Use persistent connections and timeouts:
  - HTTP keep-alive for the DeepL connection.
  - Tight timeouts (e.g., 800–1500ms) and exponential backoff on 429/5xx.
- Debounce/throttle at the UI (you’re already doing this). 150–300ms debounce typically works well for typing.
- Place the service close to DeepL and your users (region matters for round-trip time).

A minimal, fast Express implementation (DeepL + LRU + Redis + single-flight)
- Uses application/x-www-form-urlencoded (DeepL’s fastest path)
- In-memory LRU for hot hits
- Redis for cross-instance cache
- Single-flight to dedupe concurrent identical requests
- Optional array batching

```javascript
import express from 'express';
import axios from 'axios';
import crypto from 'crypto';
import LRU from 'lru-cache';
import { createClient } from 'redis';
import http from 'http';
import https from 'https';

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

// HTTP keep-alive agents
const httpAgent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 100 });
const httpsAgent = new https.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 100 });

// Axios instance tuned for low latency
const deepl = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://api.deepl.com/v2',
  timeout: 1500,
  httpAgent,
  httpsAgent,
  headers: { 'Accept-Encoding': 'gzip, deflate, br' },
});

// In-process LRU for ultra-hot hits
const lru = new LRU({
  max: 5000,
  ttl: 1000 * 60 * 10, // 10 min
});

// Redis cache for cross-instance hits
const redis = createClient({ url: 'redis://localhost:6379' });
await redis.connect();

// Single-flight map to collapse concurrent same-key requests
const inflight = new Map(); // key -> Promise

function cacheKey(text, targetLang, sourceLang) {
  const h = crypto.createHash('sha256')
    .update(`${sourceLang || 'auto'}|${targetLang}|${text}`)
    .digest('base64url');
  return `tr:${h}`;
}

async function translateDeepL({ texts, targetLang, sourceLang }) {
  // texts: array of strings (DeepL supports multiple 'text' fields)
  const params = new URLSearchParams();
  params.append('auth_key', process.env.DEEPL_API_KEY);
  for (const t of texts) params.append('text', t);
  params.append('target_lang', targetLang.toUpperCase());
  if (sourceLang) params.append('source_lang', sourceLang.toUpperCase()); // optional; omit to auto-detect

  const { data } = await deepl.post('/translate', params, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
  });

  return data.translations.map(t => t.text);
}

app.post('/api/translate', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const text = req.body.text;
    const targetLang = (req.body.target_lang || req.body.targetLang || '').toUpperCase();
    const sourceLang = req.body.source_lang || req.body.sourceLang; // optional

    if (!text || !targetLang) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Missing "text" or "target_lang"' });
    }

    // Support batch input too
    const texts = Array.isArray(text) ? text : [text];

    // Attempt batched cache resolution
    const results = new Array(texts.length);
    const lookups = [];
    const keys = texts.map(t => cacheKey(t, targetLang, sourceLang));

    // Try LRU first, then Redis
    for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
      const k = keys[i];
      const lruHit = lru.get(k);
      if (lruHit) {
        results[i] = lruHit;
      } else {
        lookups.push({ index: i, key: k });
      }
    }

    if (lookups.length) {
      const redisVals = await redis.mGet(lookups.map(x => x.key));
      for (let i = 0; i < lookups.length; i++) {
        const v = redisVals[i];
        if (v) {
          results[lookups[i].index] = v;
          lru.set(lookups[i].key, v);
        }
      }
    }

    const missing = texts
      .map((t, i) => (results[i] ? null : { i, t }))
      .filter(Boolean);

    if (missing.length) {
      // Single-flight per unique cache key set
      // For batches, we’ll fetch all missing texts in one DeepL call
      const flightKey = `batch:${keys.filter((_, i) => !results[i]).join('|')}`;

      let p = inflight.get(flightKey);
      if (!p) {
        p = (async () => {
          const missingTexts = missing.map(m => m.t);
          const translated = await translateDeepL({
            texts: missingTexts,
            targetLang,
            sourceLang, // omit for auto-detect to save time
          });

          // Cache results
          for (let j = 0; j < missing.length; j++) {
            const idx = missing[j].i;
            const k = keys[idx];
            const val = translated[j];
            results[idx] = val;
            lru.set(k, val);
            const ttlSec = 60 * 15 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 60 * 5); // 15–20 min
            await redis.setEx(k, ttlSec, val);
          }
          return true;
        })()
          .finally(() => inflight.delete(flightKey));

        inflight.set(flightKey, p);
      }
      await p;
    }

    // Return in the same shape as input
    if (Array.isArray(text)) {
      return res.json({ translations: results });
    } else {
      return res.json({ translated_text: results[0] });
    }
  } catch (err) {
    // Fast fallback (your stated requirement)
    console.error('Translation error:', err?.response?.status || err.message);
    return res.status(200).json({ translated_text: req.body.text, fallback: true });
  } finally {
    // Optional: enqueue async DB write here (don’t block the response)
    // queue.add({ source_text: text, translated_text: result, target_lang })
  }
});

app.listen(4500, () => console.log('Translation service on :4500'));
```

Notes
- This keeps most hits under 10ms (LRU/Redis) and DeepL paths ~150–220ms depending on network.
- If you must keep language detection for analytics/UX, run it asynchronously or only when translation fails to auto-detect correctly.
- Monitor p50/p95 and cache hit rates; if Redis hit rate drops, increase TTLs for the top-N phrases or pre-warm on startup.
- For very high QPS, consider HTTP/2, Redis pipelining, and a write-behind queue for DB inserts.

If you share your current handler and client usage pattern (single vs. batch, expected QPS), I can tailor the code and timeouts to your exact workload.
